WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:08.330
In March 2003, while American jets bound for
Iraq took off from Al Udeid Air Base in Qatar,

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:13.460
not fifteen miles away emerged one of the
Iraq War’s most vocal critics: the news

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.430
organization Al Jazeera.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.940
But Al Jazeera is not just any news network.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:25.500
It’s funded by the Qatari government — the
same government who invited American warplanes

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.010
to launch from its territory.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.420
The contradictions don’t stop there.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:36.370
About the last country you’d expect to lead
the charge against fossil fuels is the 4th

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.000
largest producer of natural gas.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.109
Yet Qatar spends its unimaginable oil and
gas riches sounding the alarm on climate change

00:00:45.109 --> 00:00:46.789
with Al Jazeera.

00:00:46.789 --> 00:00:52.420
Finally, who supports democracy, promotes
a progressive social agenda, and lends a voice

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:58.719
to those in need but Al Jazeera, located in
and funded by one of the least democratic,

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:03.199
least progressive, and most conservative countries
on earth.

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:08.510
In terms of political and civil liberties,
Qatar is not quite the worst.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:12.280
But, well, it’s not far from it.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:18.280
With a Freedom House score of just 25 out
of 100, it ranks behind Afghanistan, Myanmar,

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.540
and Iraq.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:25.960
In 2012, a man was sentenced to life in prison
for writing a poem that insulted the royal

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.430
family.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.810
It censors dissenting opinions online and
has been known to revoke citizenship as political

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.240
punishment.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.890
Women are expected to wear traditional dress
in public and are subject to male guardianship

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.500
laws.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.200
Non-muslims must apply for a license to buy
alcohol, are subject to a 100% sin tax, can

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.580
only do so from one authorized company, and
can only drink in private.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.540
Knowing all this, you might expect Al Jazeera
to look like Russia’s state-run RT, which

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:02.360
sows doubt and confusion, or China’s CGTN,
which is just outright propaganda.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.500
Instead, it’s more akin to NPR or the BBC.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.661
The independent watchdog “Ad Fontes Media”,
for instance, ranks all three among the most

00:02:12.661 --> 00:02:15.520
“balanced” and “reliable” sources.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.530
In fact, sometimes to their own detriment.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:24.170
Their lack of sensationalism might be characterized
by some as “bland”, “dull”, or “boring”.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:30.870
But not only is Al Jazeera relatively unbiased,
it also has some of the best, fastest, and

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.120
widest coverage of any news network on the
planet.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.800
So, why?

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:42.400
Why does one of the most conservative, non-democratic,
and, at times, repressive governments spend

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:49.130
millions upon millions on what is, essentially,
global NPR?

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:54.430
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the bonus video about why Chinese

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:59.380
propaganda fails — for just fifteen bucks
a year.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:05.290
Broadly speaking, as countries become richer
per capita, they tend to become more democratic.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.069
The more money citizens give their government
in the form of taxes, the more they demand

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:12.510
from it in return.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.660
Again, broadly speaking.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.850
Qatar, along with its neighbors, are exceptions.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:26.140
In place of taxes, they generate revenue from
oil, or, in Qatar’s case, natural gas.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:31.780
This allows the government to circumvent the
quote “normal” political relationship.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.730
Think of them less as conventional “countries”
and more as corporations whose citizens are

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.750
shareholders.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.670
Knowing they could perform a hostile takeover,
these governments essentially bribe the population

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.840
with money rather than democracy or freedom.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.950
Qatari citizens are given every luxury under
the sun — free access to American universities,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:58.790
electricity, water, healthcare, pensions,
low-cost loans, guaranteed jobs, land grants,

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.110
and even direct cash transfers.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:07.209
In 2013, its per capita GDP reached $85,000
US Dollars.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:11.849
Though, in truth, this number is wildly misleading.

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.799
Only about 300,000 of its 2.8 million residents
are citizens.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:21.549
90% are expats, who fill the jobs locals don’t
want.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:25.940
Its GDP per citizen is closer to $500,000.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:32.080
Qatar, in other words, has so few people and
so much money that it’s able to offer a

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.770
much more compelling bribe.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:40.570
As protests engulfed the region during the
2011 Arab Spring, Qatar remained a small island

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:45.160
of tranquility, despite its rule by absolute
monarchy.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.870
But besides being richer per capita, the country
fits right in among its oil-rich Middle Eastern

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:51.870
neighbors.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:58.120
It shares the same basic political and economic
model, language, religion, and culture.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.630
That’s the problem.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:05.250
Fitting in has always been what Qatar fears
most.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:11.190
In 1990, it watched Iraq’s invasion of tiny
Kuwait with a mix of panic and fear.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:17.370
It could be the next Kuwait and Saudi Arabia
its Iraq.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:24.840
With a population 14 times smaller, and an
area 186 times smaller, Qatar has always lived

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.720
in the shadow of its neighbor.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:34.400
That is, until 1995, when a coup forever changed
the course of the nation.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.000
The new emir, as its leader is known, ordered
the immediate development of what turned out

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.450
to be the world’s largest natural gas field.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.850
Before long, Qatar was rich beyond imagination.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:50.060
And with this money, it sought, in a word:
relevance.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:56.210
Since then, its foreign policy could be described
as “trying to be everything to everyone”.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:06:01.860
Whatever the conflict, discussion, organization,
or war, Qatar would position itself right

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.150
in the middle, even if that meant abandoning
all sense of logical consistency.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:11.389
Its goal was to stick out as the sore thumb
of the Middle East.

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:14.080
And stick out, it did.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.199
Since 2013, Qatar has hosted about 100 Taliban
officials at its own expense.

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:25.770
Saddam Hussein’s wife, a controversial Indian
artist in exile, and a former Chechen President

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.389
were also all offered a home in Doha.

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:32.470
At one point, it gave Assad an Airbus A340.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:38.430
And, in 1996, it became the first country
in the region to establish trade relations

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:39.910
with Israel.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:46.830
Now, around that same time, by sheer coincidence,
the BBC established a 24/7 news channel in

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.570
Saudi Arabia.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.650
But after it aired a documentary critical
of the royal family, the country withdrew

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.539
its funding.

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:59.840
For Qatar, this meant there were suddenly
hundreds of BBC-trained, Arabic-speaking,

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.920
and most importantly, unemployed journalists
next door.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.780
Seeing this opportunity, it pounced, hiring
150 of the best and brightest to start Al

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.100
Jazeera in 1996.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:19.520
The Arab world, at the time, had access to
only two categories of news: either English-language

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:25.680
reporting as told from an American or European
perspective, or locally-produced, North Korean-style

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.300
blatant propaganda.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.450
Al Jazeera, on the other hand, was refreshingly
real.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:37.090
For once, Middle Eastern viewers could hear
dissenting opinions on the issues they actually

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.020
cared about.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:44.330
By 2001, just five years after launching,
it had already become the most-watched Arab

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.419
TV news station.

00:07:47.419 --> 00:07:53.479
In 2011, it was the first or second choice
of news for 75% of the Arab world.

00:07:53.479 --> 00:07:57.289
The only thing it wasn’t doing well was
making money.

00:07:57.289 --> 00:08:04.180
Initially, the network was given a $137 million
loan to be paid back in five years.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:08.960
But its uncensored reporting soon earned it
an advertising boycott from Saudi Arabia,

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.850
Kuwait, and Bahrain, destroying all hope of
profitability.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:18.569
Yet, oddly enough, losing money, having no
obvious, tangible benefit to its financier,

00:08:18.569 --> 00:08:24.410
and being located in a country with no press
freedom all turned out to be the optimal conditions

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:27.419
for a “good” news station to thrive.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:33.430
First, because Al Jazeera eventually launched
separate English and Arabic divisions, the

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:36.630
former is given an almost infinite leash.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.700
While the Arabic version has been heavily
criticized for its whitewashed reporting on

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:46.500
Qatar and biased coverage of the region, the
English edition has near-complete editorial

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.480
independence.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:54.630
While American networks showed heavily sanitized,
video game-like footage of the wars in Afghanistan

00:08:54.630 --> 00:09:00.950
and Iraq, Al Jazeera infuriated the U.S. by
showing gory devastation and broadcasting

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.210
tapes of Bin Laden.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:09.030
This continued even after the U.S. secretary
of state asked its ally to quote “tone down”

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:13.690
its coverage — a testament to the station’s
independence.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:19.700
The entire country, including the royal family,
has paid a tremendous price for Al Jazeera’s

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:20.870
reporting.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:27.160
Starting in 2017, the governments of Saudi
Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain, and Egypt imposed

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:32.930
on it a 43-month blockade, demanding that
it shut down Al Jazeera.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:37.320
During this time, the country lost access
to its only land border, suffered supply chain

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:42.490
disruptions causing it to airlift cows from
California for milk, and was blocked from

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.690
flying over most of Middle Eastern airspace.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:51.740
Second, because the network is funded by the
government, it remains immune to market pressures,

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:56.920
allowing it to focus on under-reported stories
and avoid sensationalism.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:02.040
Instead of chasing ratings with celebrity
gossip, Al Jazeera publishes articles on humanitarian

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:07.300
crises in tiny countries and tracks elections
in little-known corners of the globe.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:12.320
At a time when viewership is dwindling and
most news networks are scaling back their

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:17.800
physical footprints, Qatar’s deep-pockets
have afforded Al Jazeera the best equipment,

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:22.080
personnel, and a staggering number of on-the-ground
stations.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.910
Its bureaus, which are spread across 50 countries,
outnumber those of CNN by 2:1.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:34.720
Third, because Qatar is, ostensibly, friends
with everyone, Al Jazeera receives unparalleled

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:36.750
access.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:41.290
Despite broadcasting pro-Palestinian coverage
in the West Bank, for instance, it somehow

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:45.170
avoided censorship from Israeli authorities.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:49.560
At the same time, its willingness to show
all sides of the story has consistently gotten

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.200
it into trouble.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.190
Its office in Gaza was destroyed by Israeli
rockets, and more than a few of its reporters

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.779
have been arrested around the world.

00:10:58.779 --> 00:11:04.870
Today, Al Jazeera is a contender for the most
reliable, highest-quality news network — on

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:11.620
any topic, in any country, with only one major
exception: Qatar.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:16.200
Twenty years ago, the country’s relative
obscurity on the world stage was an asset

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:21.720
for Al Jazeera — it could credibly claim
to have no dog in the race, no incentive to

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.230
report with bias.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:28.930
But as Al Jazeera itself has helped Qatar
emerge from the shadow of its neighbors and

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:34.800
take on a larger role in world affairs, the
network’s silence on local issues has slowly

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.089
become suspicious.

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:42.180
Its neglect of issues such as the welfare
of local migrant workers or the upcoming 2022

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:49.140
FIFA World Cup has, at last, revealed Al Jazeera’s
almost singular red-line.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:54.120
The network will host everyone from Osama
Bin Laden to Colin Powell, but it is still

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.640
not immune from self-censorship at home.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:02.320
Now, you might think the urge to intervene
in Al Jazeera’s coverage of other issues

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.950
would eventually prove irresistible to the
government.

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:10.410
But while it’s true that Al Jazeera’s
reporting is far from perfect, it may be the

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:15.390
rare instance where its incentives align with
those of the public.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.810
The greater its perception as independent,
credible, and high-quality, the better it

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:24.940
fulfills its original mission: to keep the
spotlight on Qatar.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:30.730
So far the government has shown that it understands
the long-term price for short-term control

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:35.100
and has not touched the goose that lays the
golden eggs.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:36.170
Other countries?

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:37.780
Not so much.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:41.300
China is perhaps the epitome of bad propaganda.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:45.720
Its diplomats issue death threats on Twitter
and its media channels publish a hodgepodge

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.560
of conspiracy theories and mundane press releases.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.209
Why is China so bad at telling its story?

00:12:53.209 --> 00:12:58.330
That’s the subject of this bonus video you
can watch right now on Nebula.

